---
import Section from "./Section.astro";
import Footnote from "./Footnote.astro";

const cards = [
  {
    title: "Current, not reconstructed",
    body: "AZA's standards expect daily records entered the same day or by the end of the next working day. That is a much easier bar to clear when the entry is made where the observation happened, rather than from a whiteboard at nine that evening.",
    cite: "aza",
  },
  {
    title: "Produced on demand",
    body: "An annual inventory by species with every acquisition and disposition in the period. A transfer form with identity, identifiers, provenance, medical summary and live permits. A care sheet for the enclosure door. CSV and XLSX.",
  },
  {
    title: "Able to show their own history",
    body: "Every change writes an audit row: who, what, when, and what the value was before. Deletion is soft, so a record that was removed can still account for itself. On every plan, including Starter.",
  },
];
---

<Section
  id="standards"
  eyebrow="Standards and compliance"
  title="Accreditation asks for a records system. It does not ask for ours."
  lead="AZA, the Zoological Association of America and the Global Federation of Animal Sanctuaries each require an accredited member to keep an animal records system. None of them requires a particular vendor. What they want is that the records exist, that they are current, and that you can produce them."
>
  <div class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
    <Footnote id="standards-bodies" />
  </div>

  <div class="mt-12 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-3">
    {
      cards.map((card) => (
        <article class="reveal rounded-2xl border border-border bg-card p-7 shadow-sm">
          <h3 class="text-xl text-balance">{card.title}</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
            {card.body}
            {card.cite && <Footnote id={card.cite} />}
          </p>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <div
    class="reveal mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-border border-l-4 border-l-primary bg-card p-7 sm:p-9"
  >
    <h3 class="text-xl">Two things we will not pretend to know for you</h3>

    <div class="mt-5 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <p class="font-semibold">How long you have to keep it</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground">
          The spread is wider than any single number a vendor can put on a page. Licensed exhibitors
          under the Animal Welfare Act keep records for one year after an animal is disposed of or
          dies, longer where other law applies. Federal migratory bird rehabilitation permits
          require five years. State schedules differ again.<Footnote id="retention" /> ARK never deletes
          on a schedule of its own - deletion is soft and the audit trail keeps the story - so the retention
          question stays yours to answer against the rules that actually bind you.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <p class="font-semibold">When your annual report is due</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground">
          California, Washington and New York each have their own form and their own deadline, and
          they do not agree with each other.<Footnote id="reporting" /> We are not going to publish a
          fifty-state table we cannot stand behind. What ARK gives you is the underlying data - dated,
          attributable and exportable - whichever form you are the one filling in.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>
